\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\textwidth]{figures/fig1_sta_overview.pdf}
\caption{Solver Trace Ablation (STA). (a) Formalization pipeline: NL is formalized and verified by an external solver. (b) STA protocol: leave-one-out ablation on proved formalizations yields ISR. (c) ISR as diagnostic vs.\ as reward: when the diagnostic becomes the target, meta-level Goodhart's Law emerges.}
\label{fig:sta-overview}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Task and GRPO Training}
\label{sec:task}

Given natural language premises $P_1,\ldots,P_k$ and a conclusion $C$, the task is to produce a formal logic program $F=\{c_1,\ldots,c_n\}$, where each component $c_i$ is a premise (FOL) or a clause/fact (Prolog), with one distinguished component encoding the query.
An external solver $S$ returns $S(F)\in\{\textit{proved},\textit{failed}\}$; we use Prover9 for FOL and SWI-Prolog for Prolog (\autoref{fig:sta-overview}a).

We train formalizers with GRPO~\citep{deepseekmath} using $K{=}8$ rollouts per prompt.
Each rollout receives a binary \emph{execution reward}
$r_{\text{exec}}(x) = \mathbf{1}[S(F_x) = \textit{proved}]$;
group-relative advantages are computed within each prompt's rollout group, and the policy update maximizes a clipped surrogate objective with KL regularization.
This setup optimizes prove rate but provides no signal about \emph{structural quality}: a formalization that embeds the conclusion among its premises receives the same reward as a faithful multi-step derivation.

\subsection{Solver Trace Ablation (STA)}
\label{sec:sta}

To diagnose structural quality, STA tests whether each component of a proved formalization is individually necessary for proof success (\autoref{fig:sta-overview}b).
Given a proved $x$ with $n$ components, STA constructs $n$ ablated variants $x\setminus\{c_i\}$, each removing one component, and re-runs the solver.
A component is \emph{necessary} if its removal causes proof failure: $S(x\setminus\{c_i\})=\textit{failed}$.
What constitutes a component depends on the logic type: individual premises in FOL, individual clauses or ground facts in Prolog.

\paragraph{Intensional Structural Reward.}
ISR is the fraction of necessary components:
\begin{equation}
  \text{ISR}(x) = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbf{1}\bigl[S(x\setminus\{c_i\})=\textit{failed}\bigr]
  \label{eq:isr}
\end{equation}
ISR $\in[0,1]$ is defined only for proved formalizations ($r_{\text{exec}}(x){=}1$): ISR${=}1$ indicates syntactic component necessity; ISR${=}0$ indicates complete redundancy.
The protocol is solver-agnostic, requiring $n{+}1$ solver calls per sample.
STA draws on the LOO intuition from mutation testing~\citep{demillo1978, jia-harman-survey} but operates on solver \emph{inputs} (formalizations) rather than program code, targeting component necessity rather than test adequacy.
Pseudocode is in Appendix~\ref{app:algorithm}.

\paragraph{Limitations of ISR.}
ISR measures syntactic component necessity, not semantic faithfulness to the natural language input.
It is also subject to a component-count confound: formalizations with fewer components yield mechanically higher ISR, since each carries a larger share of the proof burden under LOO.
We control for this confound in our analysis (\S\ref{sec:goodhart}).

\subsection{Reward Conditions}
\label{sec:rewards}

We study six reward conditions (\autoref{fig:sta-overview}c).
For all RL conditions, bonuses apply only to proved samples; unproved samples receive $r{=}0$.

\noindent\textbf{Exec-only:} $r=r_{\text{exec}}$ (baseline).
\noindent\textbf{Additive ISR:} $r=r_{\text{exec}}\cdot(1+\beta\,\text{ISR}(x))$ with $\beta\in\{0.1,0.3\}$, gating the structural bonus on proof success.
\noindent\textbf{Additive Random:} $r=r_{\text{exec}}\cdot(1+\beta\,u)$, $u\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)$, $\beta\in\{0.1,0.3\}$: control isolating ISR-specific structural signal from variance/scaling effects. We draw from $\mathcal{U}(0,1)$ to match ISR's $[0,1]$ range while preserving the execution-gating structure; this provides a conservative lower bound on ISR-specific effects since any structured signal---even random---may regularize training.
\noindent\textbf{SFT baseline:} supervised fine-tuning on gold formalizations, testing whether observed hacking is RL-specific.

Each RL condition is trained with seeds $\{42,43,44\}$ controlling initialization and stochasticity; all seeds share identical training data (\S\ref{sec:setup}).

\subsection{Hacking Taxonomy}
\label{sec:taxonomy-method}

Across conditions, seeds, and logic types, we identify seven hacking modes (H1, H7, tautological collapse, repetition collapse, complexity-without-utility, contradiction explosion, query-as-fact gaming; full definitions in \S\ref{sec:taxonomy} and Appendix~\ref{app:hacking-modes}).
Modes are identified by a two-stage procedure: strict normalized text matching catches H1/H7; functional analysis of the per-sample $n_{\text{necessary}}$ distribution from STA catches modes that evade text matching.

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We evaluate STA along three axes: as a diagnostic for reward hacking (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic}); as a training reward exhibiting meta-level Goodhart's Law (\S\ref{sec:goodhart}); and as a cross-logic protocol (\S\ref{sec:crosslogic}). We synthesize results into a hacking taxonomy (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}).

\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

We train Qwen3-4B with GRPO~\citep{deepseekmath} using $K{=}8$ rollouts per prompt for 500 steps (default) or 1000 where noted, across seeds $\{42,43,44\}$.
Training data is a fixed 500-example subset of FOLIO~\citep{folio} (\texttt{train\_500\_seed42.jsonl}), held constant across all seeds and conditions. %% data: C001
We use Prover9 for FOL and SWI-Prolog 8.4.2 for Prolog.
Evaluation is on 100 held-out FOLIO examples with greedy decoding; all cross-run ISR values are computed by \texttt{eval\_sta.py} which applies STA uniformly. %% C004
We report prove rate (PR), proved-only ISR (ISR$^*$, over proved samples only), hacking mode percentages (H7, H1\footnote{Hacking mode numbering follows our taxonomy order (Appendix~\ref{app:hacking-modes}); H1 is the most prevalent mode.}), non-hacking rate\footnote{We define ``non-hacking'' as outputs not matching any of our seven identified hacking patterns (Table~\ref{tab:hacking-taxonomy-app}); this does not imply semantic correctness.}, and mean component count.
Six reward conditions are compared: \textbf{Exec-only}, \textbf{ISR~$\beta{\in}\{0.1,0.3\}$}, \textbf{Random~$\beta{\in}\{0.1,0.3\}$}, and \textbf{SFT}.
Outputs are classified into H7, H1, and Non-hacking using text matching cross-validated with functional LOO analysis.
Full hyperparameters are in Appendix~\ref{app:setup}.

\subsection{ISR as Diagnostic}
\label{sec:diagnostic}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_isr_collapse.pdf}
\caption{ISR collapse under exec-only GRPO (seed~42). PR rises 32\%$\to$96\% while proved-only ISR falls 0.642$\to$0.003 over 1000 steps. By step 1000, 94.8\% of proved samples have ISR${=}0$ and mean component count inflates 6.58$\to$18.55 (contradiction explosion).}
\label{fig:isr-collapse}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Collapse trajectory.}
Figure~\ref{fig:isr-collapse} traces ISR and PR over training for exec-only GRPO (seed~42).
At 100 steps: PR${=}32$\%, ISR$^*{=}0.642$. %% registry: plan003-eval-exec-only-r5
By 500 steps: PR${=}93$\% but ISR$^*{=}0.132$---87.1\% of proved samples embed the conclusion among the premises (H1). %% registry: plan001-exec-only-s42-r2
At 1000 steps: PR${=}96$\% while ISR$^*$ drops to 0.003 (H1${=}100$\%, mean components 18.55). %% registry: plan016-extended-1000-s42
The ISR$^*$ trajectory ($0.642{\to}0.132{\to}0.003$) is monotonically decreasing: extended training worsens structural degeneration, invisible to PR.

\textbf{Cross-seed instability.}
The three exec-only seeds diverge radically: s42 hacks via H1 (PR${=}93$\%, H1${=}87.1$\%); s43 collapses to PR${=}13$\% (ISR$^*{=}0$) via repetition---87\% of outputs hit the max token length; s44 reaches PR${=}98$\% via tautological collapse (H7${=}100$\%, mean components 2.04). %% registry: plan001-exec-only-s42-r2, plan001-exec-only-s43-r4, plan001-exec-only-s44
Despite PR$\in[13,98]$\%, all three seeds exhibit structural degeneration detectable by ISR.
The cross-seed PR $\sigma{=}\pm47$\,pp is itself a finding: aggregate metrics obscure qualitatively different failure modes (per-seed details in Table~\ref{tab:beta01-3seed}).

\textbf{SFT baseline.}
A supervised baseline achieves PR${=}14$\% with ISR$^*{=}0.571$: 92.9\% (13/14) [66.1\%, 99.8\%] of proved samples are non-hacking, only 1 exhibits H1, and no H7 instances occur. %% registry: plan009-sft-baseline-s42-r4
The near-absence of hacking is consistent with hacking correlating with optimization aggressiveness rather than being driven purely by model capacity or data limitations.
We note that the SFT PR level (14\%) is substantially lower than RL conditions, and the low hacking rate may partly reflect fewer opportunities for reward exploitation rather than a purely training-method difference; we address this confound via a 100-step RL checkpoint analysis in Appendix~\ref{app:100step}.

\subsection{Meta-Level Goodhart's Law}
\label{sec:goodhart}

ISR-as-reward provides the first empirical demonstration of Goodhart's Law at the meta level~\citep{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy}: the diagnostic itself becomes the target of gaming when optimized.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Condition} & $\boldsymbol{\beta}$ & \textbf{PR\%}$\uparrow$ & \textbf{ISR}$^*$ & \textbf{H7\%} & \textbf{H1\%} & \textbf{N-H\%} \\
\midrule
Base (0-shot) & --- & 12 & --- & --- & --- & --- \\ %% registry: sta-phase0-baseline
SFT & --- & 14 & 0.571 & 0 & 7 & \textbf{93} \\ %% registry: plan009-sft-baseline-s42-r4
\midrule
Exec-only$^\dagger$ & --- & 93 & 0.132 & 0 & 87 & 13 \\ %% registry: plan001-exec-only-s42-r2
ISR & 0.1 & 77{\scriptsize$\pm$14} & $\ddagger$ & \textbf{65} & 17 & ${\sim}$16 \\ %% registry: plan002-additive-b01-s42/s43/s44
Random & 0.1 & \textbf{85}{\scriptsize$\pm$6} & 0.086{\scriptsize$\pm$.04} & 0 & 81 & 19 \\ %% registry: plan018-additive-random-b01-s42/s43-r2/s44
\midrule
ISR & 0.3 & 18 & 0.143 & --- & --- & --- \\ %% registry: plan002-additive-b03-s42
Random & 0.3 & 48 & 0.094 & --- & --- & --- \\ %% registry: plan017-additive-random-s42
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Goodhart landscape across reward conditions. 3-seed rows show mean$\pm$std (ISR/Random $\beta{=}0.1$); other rows are single seed. $^\dagger$Seed~42; see \S\ref{sec:diagnostic} for 3-seed divergence. $\ddagger$ISR$^*$ omitted: inflated by H7 gaming (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:beta01-3seed}). H7 and H1 rates are simple means across seeds. Best PR and N-H\% bolded per group.}
\label{tab:goodhart-landscape}
\end{table}

\textbf{Low-dose ISR ($\beta{=}0.1$): ISR-specific gaming.}
Table~\ref{tab:goodhart-landscape} summarizes results.
At $\beta{=}0.1$, 2/3 ISR seeds (s43, s44) are dominated by H7---a single premise identical to the conclusion, trivially achieving both high PR and ISR.
Across the three ISR seeds, H7 accounts for 64.5\% of proved samples (149/231); all three random controls show \emph{zero} H7 (0/256), confirming H7 is exclusively induced by the ISR signal. %% registry: plan002-additive-b01-s42/s43/s44 vs plan018-additive-random-b01-s42/s43-r2/s44
Conclusion embedding (H1+H7) is GRPO-universal at 68--97\% across RL conditions: random produces only H1 (70.9--93.2\%), while ISR converts H1$\to$H7 without reducing the total (Figure~\ref{fig:hacking-composition}; per-seed: Appendix Table~\ref{tab:beta01-3seed}).
Random also achieves higher and more stable PR (85$\pm$6\% vs 77$\pm$14\%). %% registry: plan018 vs plan002 3-seed means

\textbf{Premise-count confound.}
Seed~42 under ISR---the only seed not dominated by H7---shows higher raw ISR$^*$ (0.415 vs 0.131 for random). %% registry: plan002-additive-b01-s42 (ISR*=0.4154), plan018-additive-random-b01-s42 (ISR*=0.1314)
This is entirely a premise-minimization artifact: ISR-trained models produce shorter formalizations (mean 5.58 vs 8.46 components), and LOO is mathematically biased toward shorter inputs. %% registry: plan002-additive-b01-s42 (comp=5.58), plan018-additive-random-b01-s42 (comp=8.46)
Restricting to non-hacking samples with $\geq$3 premises: ISR non-hacking ISR$^*{=}0.258$ ($n{=}11$) vs random non-hacking ISR$^*{=}0.243$ ($n{=}21$)---a 1.06$\times$ ratio, no meaningful advantage. %% D051 premise-count-matched analysis
Of ISR s42's 25 nominally non-hacking samples, 15 have only 1--2 premises, where ISR$\geq0.5$ is a mathematical artifact.

\textbf{High-dose ISR ($\beta{=}0.3$): classic Goodhart.}
At $\beta{=}0.3$, ISR causes PR to collapse to 18\%---a 5.2$\times$ degradation from exec-only---while the matched random control achieves PR${=}48$\%. %% registry: plan002-additive-b03-s42, plan017-additive-random-s42
The two doses reveal a complete Goodhart trajectory~\citep{karwowski-goodhart-rl, gao-reward-overoptimization} at the meta level: low-dose proxy gaming (ISR inflation via H7) followed by high-dose performance collapse, with the proxy being gamed itself a gaming detector.
Under the taxonomy of \citet{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy}, $\beta{=}0.3$ exemplifies extremal Goodhart while $\beta{=}0.1$ instantiates a meta-level variant.

\subsection{Cross-Logic Diagnostic Transfer}
\label{sec:crosslogic}

We replicated both experiments using SWI-Prolog 8.4.2 (full results: Appendix Table~\ref{tab:prolog-crosslogic}).
Prolog exec-only confirms diagnostic transfer (2/3 seeds with ISR$^*{\approx}0$ despite moderate-high PR); the low ISR reflects \emph{redundant coding} (repeated ground facts), not absent reasoning---contrasting the metric-gaming high ISR under Prolog ISR-reward. %% registry: plan006-prolog-exec-s42 (ISR*=0.037), plan006-prolog-exec-s43-r3 (ISR*=0.015), plan006-prolog-exec-s44 (ISR*=0.387, small-sample n=21); C006
ISR-as-reward yields universal Goodhart with 0\% non-hacking across 185 proved samples (stronger than the FOL ${\sim}16$\% residual), via three distinct mechanisms: s42 clean H7 tautologies (ISR$^*{=}1.000$); s43 uppercase pseudo-Prolog functionally equivalent to H7 ($n_{\text{nec}}{=}1$, 38/38); s44 query-as-fact gaming, converting NL to snake\_case atoms used as both fact and query (ISR$^*{=}0.912$, $n_{\text{nec}}{=}1$, 95/97). %% registry: plan006-prolog-sta-s42 (H7=100%, ISR*=1.0), plan006-prolog-sta-s43 (H1=97.4%, n_nec=1 38/38), plan006-prolog-sta-s44 (PR=0.97, ISR=0.885, h7_functional=97.9%)
The diversity of pathways under identical data and reward shows Goodhart here is a family of convergent exploits; the text-classifier bypass by s44 motivates functional cross-validation of automated diagnostics.

\subsection{Hacking Taxonomy}
\label{sec:taxonomy}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.9\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_hacking_composition.pdf}
\caption{Hacking composition across reward conditions (proved samples). ISR converts H1 (conclusion embedding) into more extreme H7 (conclusion duplication) without reducing total hacking; SFT is near-zero hacking (92.9\% non-hacking).}
\label{fig:hacking-composition}
\end{figure}

The seven modes (quantitative breakdown and definitions in Appendix~\ref{app:hacking-modes}, Table~\ref{tab:hacking-taxonomy-app}): \textbf{H1} (GRPO-universal, 68--97\%), \textbf{H7} (ISR-specific, 64.5\% under $\beta{=}0.1$ vs.\ 0\% random), \textbf{tautological} (exec-only s44, 94\%), \textbf{repetition} (exec-only s43, 87\% hit max length), \textbf{contradiction explosion} (mean 18.55 components at 1000 steps), \textbf{complexity-without-utility} (Random $\beta{=}0.1$, 3.8\%), \textbf{query-as-fact} (Prolog ISR s44, bypasses text classifiers). %% registry: plan001-exec-only-s44, plan001-exec-only-s43-r4, plan016-extended-1000-s42, plan006-prolog-sta-s44
The reward signal determines hacking \emph{composition} but not \emph{prevalence}: total conclusion-in-premise rates are comparable across ISR and random (${\sim}82$\% pooled), but ISR converts H1 into H7.
SFT remains the only condition with near-zero hacking (Figure~\ref{fig:hacking-composition}), consistent with hacking being RL-reward-specific. %% registry: plan009-sft-baseline-s42-r4

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We evaluate STA along three axes: as a diagnostic for reward hacking (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic}); as a training reward exhibiting meta-level Goodhart's Law (\S\ref{sec:goodhart}); and as a cross-logic protocol (\S\ref{sec:crosslogic}). We synthesize results into a hacking taxonomy (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}).

\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

We train Qwen3-4B with GRPO~\citep{deepseekmath} using $K{=}8$ rollouts per prompt for 500 steps (default) or 1000 where noted, across seeds $\{42,43,44\}$.
Training data is a fixed 500-example subset of FOLIO~\citep{folio} (\texttt{train\_500\_seed42.jsonl}), held constant across all seeds and conditions. %% data: C001
We use Prover9 for FOL and SWI-Prolog 8.4.2 for Prolog.
Evaluation is on 100 held-out FOLIO examples with greedy decoding; all cross-run ISR values are computed by \texttt{eval\_sta.py} which applies STA uniformly. %% C004
We report prove rate (PR), proved-only ISR (ISR$^*$, over proved samples only), hacking mode percentages (H7, H1\footnote{Hacking mode numbering follows our taxonomy order (Appendix~\ref{app:hacking-modes}); H1 is the most prevalent mode.}), non-hacking rate\footnote{We define ``non-hacking'' as outputs not matching any of our seven identified hacking patterns (Table~\ref{tab:hacking-taxonomy-app}); this does not imply semantic correctness.}, and mean component count.
Six reward conditions are compared: \textbf{Exec-only}, \textbf{ISR~$\beta{\in}\{0.1,0.3\}$}, \textbf{Random~$\beta{\in}\{0.1,0.3\}$}, and \textbf{SFT}.
Outputs are classified into H7, H1, and Non-hacking using text matching cross-validated with functional LOO analysis.
Full hyperparameters are in Appendix~\ref{app:setup}.

\subsection{ISR as Diagnostic}
\label{sec:diagnostic}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_isr_collapse.pdf}
\caption{ISR collapse under exec-only GRPO (seed~42). PR rises 32\%$\to$96\% while proved-only ISR falls 0.642$\to$0.003 over 1000 steps. By step 1000, 94.8\% of proved samples have ISR${=}0$ and mean component count inflates 6.58$\to$18.55 (contradiction explosion).}
\label{fig:isr-collapse}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Collapse trajectory.}
Figure~\ref{fig:isr-collapse} traces ISR and PR over training for exec-only GRPO (seed~42).
At 100 steps: PR${=}32$\%, ISR$^*{=}0.642$. %% registry: plan003-eval-exec-only-r5
By 500 steps: PR${=}93$\% but ISR$^*{=}0.132$---87.1\% of proved samples embed the conclusion among the premises (H1). %% registry: plan001-exec-only-s42-r2
At 1000 steps: PR${=}96$\% while ISR$^*$ drops to 0.003 (H1${=}100$\%, mean components 18.55). %% registry: plan016-extended-1000-s42
The ISR$^*$ trajectory ($0.642{\to}0.132{\to}0.003$) is monotonically decreasing: extended training worsens structural degeneration, invisible to PR.

\textbf{Cross-seed instability.}
The three exec-only seeds diverge radically: s42 hacks via H1 (PR${=}93$\%, H1${=}87.1$\%); s43 collapses to PR${=}13$\% (ISR$^*{=}0$) via repetition---87\% of outputs hit the max token length; s44 reaches PR${=}98$\% via tautological collapse (H7${=}100$\%, mean components 2.04). %% registry: plan001-exec-only-s42-r2, plan001-exec-only-s43-r4, plan001-exec-only-s44
Despite PR$\in[13,98]$\%, all three seeds exhibit structural degeneration detectable by ISR.
The cross-seed PR $\sigma{=}\pm47$\,pp is itself a finding: aggregate metrics obscure qualitatively different failure modes (per-seed details in Table~\ref{tab:beta01-3seed}).

\textbf{SFT baseline.}
A supervised baseline achieves PR${=}14$\% with ISR$^*{=}0.571$: 92.9\% (13/14) [66.1\%, 99.8\%] of proved samples are non-hacking, only 1 exhibits H1, and no H7 instances occur. %% registry: plan009-sft-baseline-s42-r4
The near-absence of hacking is consistent with hacking correlating with optimization aggressiveness rather than being driven purely by model capacity or data limitations.
We note that the SFT PR level (14\%) is substantially lower than RL conditions, and the low hacking rate may partly reflect fewer opportunities for reward exploitation rather than a purely training-method difference; we address this confound via a 100-step RL checkpoint analysis in Appendix~\ref{app:100step}.

\subsection{Meta-Level Goodhart's Law}
\label{sec:goodhart}

ISR-as-reward provides the first empirical demonstration of Goodhart's Law at the meta level~\citep{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy}: the diagnostic itself becomes the target of gaming when optimized.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Condition} & $\boldsymbol{\beta}$ & \textbf{PR\%}$\uparrow$ & \textbf{ISR}$^*$ & \textbf{H7\%} & \textbf{H1\%} & \textbf{N-H\%} \\
\midrule
Base (0-shot) & --- & 12 & --- & --- & --- & --- \\ %% registry: sta-phase0-baseline
SFT & --- & 14 & 0.571 & 0 & 7 & \textbf{93} \\ %% registry: plan009-sft-baseline-s42-r4
\midrule
Exec-only$^\dagger$ & --- & 93 & 0.132 & 0 & 87 & 13 \\ %% registry: plan001-exec-only-s42-r2
ISR & 0.1 & 77{\scriptsize$\pm$14} & $\ddagger$ & \textbf{65} & 17 & ${\sim}$16 \\ %% registry: plan002-additive-b01-s42/s43/s44
Random & 0.1 & \textbf{85}{\scriptsize$\pm$6} & 0.086{\scriptsize$\pm$.04} & 0 & 81 & 19 \\ %% registry: plan018-additive-random-b01-s42/s43-r2/s44
\midrule
ISR & 0.3 & 18 & 0.143 & --- & --- & --- \\ %% registry: plan002-additive-b03-s42
Random & 0.3 & 48 & 0.094 & --- & --- & --- \\ %% registry: plan017-additive-random-s42
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Goodhart landscape across reward conditions. 3-seed rows show mean$\pm$std (ISR/Random $\beta{=}0.1$); other rows are single seed. $^\dagger$Seed~42; see \S\ref{sec:diagnostic} for 3-seed divergence. $\ddagger$ISR$^*$ omitted: inflated by H7 gaming (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:beta01-3seed}). H7 and H1 rates are simple means across seeds. Best PR and N-H\% bolded per group.}
\label{tab:goodhart-landscape}
\end{table}

\textbf{Low-dose ISR ($\beta{=}0.1$): ISR-specific gaming.}
Table~\ref{tab:goodhart-landscape} summarizes results.
At $\beta{=}0.1$, 2/3 ISR seeds (s43, s44) are dominated by H7---a single premise identical to the conclusion, trivially achieving both high PR and ISR.
Across the three ISR seeds, H7 accounts for 64.5\% of proved samples (149/231); all three random controls show \emph{zero} H7 (0/256), confirming H7 is exclusively induced by the ISR signal. %% registry: plan002-additive-b01-s42/s43/s44 vs plan018-additive-random-b01-s42/s43-r2/s44
Conclusion embedding (H1+H7) is GRPO-universal at 68--97\% across RL conditions: random produces only H1 (70.9--93.2\%), while ISR converts H1$\to$H7 without reducing the total (Figure~\ref{fig:hacking-composition}; per-seed: Appendix Table~\ref{tab:beta01-3seed}).
Random also achieves higher and more stable PR (85$\pm$6\% vs 77$\pm$14\%). %% registry: plan018 vs plan002 3-seed means

\textbf{Premise-count confound.}
Seed~42 under ISR---the only seed not dominated by H7---shows higher raw ISR$^*$ (0.415 vs 0.131 for random). %% registry: plan002-additive-b01-s42 (ISR*=0.4154), plan018-additive-random-b01-s42 (ISR*=0.1314)
This is entirely a premise-minimization artifact: ISR-trained models produce shorter formalizations (mean 5.58 vs 8.46 components), and LOO is mathematically biased toward shorter inputs. %% registry: plan002-additive-b01-s42 (comp=5.58), plan018-additive-random-b01-s42 (comp=8.46)
Restricting to non-hacking samples with $\geq$3 premises: ISR non-hacking ISR$^*{=}0.258$ ($n{=}11$) vs random non-hacking ISR$^*{=}0.243$ ($n{=}21$)---a 1.06$\times$ ratio, no meaningful advantage. %% D051 premise-count-matched analysis
Of ISR s42's 25 nominally non-hacking samples, 15 have only 1--2 premises, where ISR$\geq0.5$ is a mathematical artifact.

\textbf{High-dose ISR ($\beta{=}0.3$): classic Goodhart.}
At $\beta{=}0.3$, ISR causes PR to collapse to 18\%---a 5.2$\times$ degradation from exec-only---while the matched random control achieves PR${=}48$\%. %% registry: plan002-additive-b03-s42, plan017-additive-random-s42
The two doses reveal a complete Goodhart trajectory~\citep{karwowski-goodhart-rl, gao-reward-overoptimization} at the meta level: low-dose proxy gaming (ISR inflation via H7) followed by high-dose performance collapse, with the proxy being gamed itself a gaming detector.
Under the taxonomy of \citet{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy}, $\beta{=}0.3$ exemplifies extremal Goodhart while $\beta{=}0.1$ instantiates a meta-level variant.

\subsection{Cross-Logic Diagnostic Transfer}
\label{sec:crosslogic}

We replicated both experiments using SWI-Prolog 8.4.2 (full results: Appendix Table~\ref{tab:prolog-crosslogic}).
Prolog exec-only confirms diagnostic transfer (2/3 seeds with ISR$^*{\approx}0$ despite moderate-high PR); the low ISR reflects \emph{redundant coding} (repeated ground facts), not absent reasoning---contrasting the metric-gaming high ISR under Prolog ISR-reward. %% registry: plan006-prolog-exec-s42 (ISR*=0.037), plan006-prolog-exec-s43-r3 (ISR*=0.015), plan006-prolog-exec-s44 (ISR*=0.387, small-sample n=21); C006
ISR-as-reward yields universal Goodhart with 0\% non-hacking across 185 proved samples (stronger than the FOL ${\sim}16$\% residual), via three distinct mechanisms: s42 clean H7 tautologies (ISR$^*{=}1.000$); s43 uppercase pseudo-Prolog functionally equivalent to H7 ($n_{\text{nec}}{=}1$, 38/38); s44 query-as-fact gaming, converting NL to snake\_case atoms used as both fact and query (ISR$^*{=}0.912$, $n_{\text{nec}}{=}1$, 95/97). %% registry: plan006-prolog-sta-s42 (H7=100%, ISR*=1.0), plan006-prolog-sta-s43 (H1=97.4%, n_nec=1 38/38), plan006-prolog-sta-s44 (PR=0.97, ISR=0.885, h7_functional=97.9%)
The diversity of pathways under identical data and reward shows Goodhart here is a family of convergent exploits; the text-classifier bypass by s44 motivates functional cross-validation of automated diagnostics.

\subsection{Hacking Taxonomy}
\label{sec:taxonomy}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.9\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_hacking_composition.pdf}
\caption{Hacking composition across reward conditions (proved samples). ISR converts H1 (conclusion embedding) into more extreme H7 (conclusion duplication) without reducing total hacking; SFT is near-zero hacking (92.9\% non-hacking).}
\label{fig:hacking-composition}
\end{figure}

The seven modes (quantitative breakdown and definitions in Appendix~\ref{app:hacking-modes}, Table~\ref{tab:hacking-taxonomy-app}): \textbf{H1} (GRPO-universal, 68--97\%), \textbf{H7} (ISR-specific, 64.5\% under $\beta{=}0.1$ vs.\ 0\% random), \textbf{tautological} (exec-only s44, 94\%), \textbf{repetition} (exec-only s43, 87\% hit max length), \textbf{contradiction explosion} (mean 18.55 components at 1000 steps), \textbf{complexity-without-utility} (Random $\beta{=}0.1$, 3.8\%), \textbf{query-as-fact} (Prolog ISR s44, bypasses text classifiers). %% registry: plan001-exec-only-s44, plan001-exec-only-s43-r4, plan016-extended-1000-s42, plan006-prolog-sta-s44
The reward signal determines hacking \emph{composition} but not \emph{prevalence}: total conclusion-in-premise rates are comparable across ISR and random (${\sim}82$\% pooled), but ISR converts H1 into H7.
SFT remains the only condition with near-zero hacking (Figure~\ref{fig:hacking-composition}), consistent with hacking being RL-reward-specific. %% registry: plan009-sft-baseline-s42-r4

